WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.460
- Red telephone boxes like this one
are an icon of Britain,

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.040
or, well, they were.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.030
Nowadays, the boxes that are left
are falling into disrepair and ruin

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:10.740
because there's not much demand
for payphones these days.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.250
If a little village wants to save theirs,

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:13.827
then the phone company will
sell it to them for £1,

00:00:13.827 --> 00:00:16.000
enough to make the
contract legal and binding,

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.510
but they won't keep the phone connected.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.920
So what do you do with
a disused phone box?

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.610
Well, one of the options
being installed right here

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.620
might help to save lives.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.870
Community Heartbeat Trust
is currently the leading organisation

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.490
for the provision of community
defibrillators in the UK.

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:36.050
Today we've probably positioned 6,500
community defibrillators or thereabouts.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.680
It costs somewhere between
around £1600 and £2200,

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:40.820
depending on the equipment.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:42.960
- It turns out the logistics
aren't quite as simple as

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.560
"buy a defibrillator
and stick it in a box".

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:46.970
A defibrillator is a medical device,

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:51.120
if it's poorly maintained or it breaks,
then it could be worse than useless.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.430
It could actually do harm.

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:55.200
- There is no use just putting it in
and then forgetting about it.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.370
These need to be looked after

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:58.370
and it's got to be as good
in several years' time

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.930
as it is on day one.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:01.200
And it's got to be sustainable,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.310
it's not just about
raising funds initially,

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:06.190
it's also about ongoing costs
and ongoing fundraising

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.540
to make sure that this
is actually looked after

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:11.410
for the entire life that it's going
to be in the community.

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:13.430
Well, the first thing we need
to find out from the process is:

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:15.730
is the kiosk actually available?

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:16.950
They can't decommission it
unless there's

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.320
a mobile phone signal
for emergencies.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.480
When a community contacts us,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.920
we'll let the local ambulance service know
that a defibrillator is going in,

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.420
and then it's available in an emergency.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:28.717
When somebody phones 999, the
ambulance service will be able to say

00:01:28.717 --> 00:01:29.829
"There is a defibrillator,

00:01:29.829 --> 00:01:33.010
"it's located at the phone box,
on the corner of X and Y roads."

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.760
And if the cabinet is locked,
they'll give an access code.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.110
Once the defibrillator is used,

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.660
we just take out the old pads,
put on new ones,

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.480
run a self check to make sure the
defibrillator is working properly,

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.110
and then it's ready for action again.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.620
- And there's another question:
who pays for the power?

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.390
The box is connected to the national grid

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:51.100
so the light and the phone could work
back when it was an actual phone box,

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.160
but it doesn't have an electricity meter.
It's never needed one.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.070
- The electricity is there to provide
electricity for the cabinet

00:01:58.070 --> 00:01:59.640
because there's a heater in the cabinet.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.600
It's not for the defibrillator,
the defibrillators all work off batteries.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.440
And the heater in the cabinets

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.870
keeps the defibrillator above
freezing in cold weather.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:07.890
That's really quite important

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.860
because batteries do
degrade in cold weather.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.270
The electrodes are water gel
so they will freeze.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:15.010
British Telecom, who's the
major phone company in the UK,

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.930
are the people who actually
originally own these.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:20.070
It actually costs BT money
to take out the electricity.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.110
So they would rather
leave the electricity in,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.980
what they do with us is: they
donate that to us as charity

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.990
and then we provide the
electricity to the community.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:31.500
So the estimated cost for a kiosk
to run it for a year is about £5-10.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.260
- All of this might sound good in theory.
But the question is:

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:40.200
could a bystander with no medical training
actually use one of these to save a life?

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.890
To answer that, I've come
to the headquarters

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.690
of the East of England
Ambulance Service

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:45.640
who are going to let me try their
training defibrillator.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.920
It doesn't actually produce a shock,
we just need to use it on a dummy,

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.160
but it is as realistic a simulation
as it's possible to get.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.007
So here's the story.

00:02:52.007 --> 00:02:54.290
Someone has collapsed,
someone else is doing CPR,

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:57.180
and I've been sent by the
emergency operator to get this.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.000
- So the very first thing
you do is turn it on

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.720
and listen to the voice prompts.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.420
It will tell you to follow it calmly,
which is a really important thing to do.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.677
- So we assume there's
someone here doing CPR,

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.083
and now I have to take this...

00:03:10.083 --> 00:03:12.861
On/Off. [defibrillator beeps]

00:03:12.861 --> 00:03:16.580
- "Call Emergency Medical Services now."
- Okay, we've done that.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:18.463
- "Adult mode."
- Yes.

00:03:18.463 --> 00:03:21.370
- "Follow the voice prompt calmly."
- Okay.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:24.047
- "Remove all clothing from
chest and stomach."

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.030
- It's really important that
all of the clothing is removed,

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:29.270
we want a bare chest.

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:33.070
There are concerns over
maintaining dignity...

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.500
life comes before dignity.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:37.840
- Take out the pads from the
bottom of the device, okay.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.970
- The next thing it'll ask you is
to apply the pads to the chest.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.800
So the first pad, you're going to put
on the patient's right shoulder,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.580
the second pad you're going to put
just below their left armpit.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:47.470
- Just... there.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:50.069
- "Peel off the pad labelled 2."
- Okay.

00:03:50.069 --> 00:03:52.860
- "And stick to the bare
skin of the patient,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.489
"exactly as shown in the picture."

00:03:54.489 --> 00:03:57.956
- Exactly as shown in the
picture, just below there. Okay.

00:03:57.956 --> 00:03:58.789
[defibrillator beeps]

00:03:58.789 --> 00:04:00.734
- "Do not touch the patient."

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.850
"Analysing heart rhythm."

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.310
- At this point, it will tell
you not to touch the patient,

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:06.320
and that's really important,

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.210
it's going to be analysing
that heart rhythm.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:10.000
One of the ones it's
going to be looking for

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.820
is where the heart is
just wobbling around,

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:14.790
where there's no regularity to it
and it is not able to pump blood.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.120
What it needs to do then,

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.530
is stop that heart with an electric shock.

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:21.550
Once it's stopped it, the
natural pacemaker of the heart

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:24.310
can kick back in and put the
heart back into a normal rhythm

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:25.360
where it can pump blood.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:26.988
- "Shock advised."

00:04:26.988 --> 00:04:27.821
- Okay.

00:04:27.821 --> 00:04:29.518
[defibrillator beeping]
- "Stand clear."

00:04:29.518 --> 00:04:32.077
"Press the flashing orange button now."

00:04:32.077 --> 00:04:32.782
[beep stops]

00:04:32.782 --> 00:04:34.330
"Shock delivered."

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:35.463
[defibrillator beeping]

00:04:35.463 --> 00:04:38.250
"Begin CPR now."

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:42.070
- If they are in a rhythm
that the AED can shock

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:45.393
it will increase their chance
of survival about 10% per minute.

00:04:45.393 --> 00:04:46.303
[rhythmic clicking]

00:04:46.303 --> 00:04:48.000
- "Reanalyzing heart rhythm...

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.286
"...in one minute."
- Oh.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:52.830
- So I've arrived at a call
where there is a defib,

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.210
it has been used and it's
been used successfully.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:57.320
And the patient has
started breathing again

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:58.662
prior to my arrival.

00:04:58.662 --> 00:05:01.020
- "Analysing heart rhythm."

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:04.510
- It is the human equivalent of
turning someone off then on again.

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.360
- "Shock advised."

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:09.080
- If you haven't had any form of training,
you can still use a defibrillator,

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:10.830
you shouldn't be prevented from doing it,

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:11.780
but you should be trained

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.793
in order to get the most
efficient usage of that equipment.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:20.370
- So I assume this would continue until
emergency services actually arrive?

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.600
- So yes, once we arrive,
we'll take over what you're doing.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.030
We'll attach our defibrillator and
take over management of the patient.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:30.980
- I realised when it said "start CPR",
that I don't actually know about that,

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:33.340
I only know what I've
picked up from media?

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:35.540
So I'm hoping I was vaguely
in the right position there.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:37.140
- Yep, absolutely.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.951
- I need to go do proper
first aid training.

00:05:39.784 --> 00:05:40.617
Thank you!

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:43.620
- Ideally, it could have done 

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.720
with just being up a bit on
the shoulder rather than there,

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:46.655
but, you know,
that would have worked.

00:05:46.655 --> 00:05:47.490
- That would work.
- The main thing is,

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.363
that would have done the job.
- Right.

00:05:49.363 --> 00:05:50.780
- You were in the centre
of the chest there,

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:53.530
doing the chest compressions,
right speed. It was fine.

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:54.940
You would have saved their life,

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:57.030
if it was saveable,
you would have done it.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:58.430
Which is all that matters.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:05:59.653
- Thank you very much!

